    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID43",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_3542ae7e3ded23beb7969fb0__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">2. SOCIETAL CONTEXT AND ROOT CAUSES OF CYBER VIOLENCE AND HATE SPEECH ONLINE AGAINST WOMEN &gt; 2.3. The architecture of cyber spaces and women’s victimisation &gt; 2.3.3. Mob mentality\n\nThey include rape and death threats — often credible ones — and frequently involve real-life targeting, which adds to the credibility of the threats;\n\nThey cross multiple social media or online platforms;\n\nThey occur at unusually high levels of intensity and frequency (numerous threats or messages per day or even per hour);\n\nThey are perpetuated for an unusual duration (months or even years);\n\nThey involve many attackers in a concerted and often coordinated campaign.\n\nDanielle Keats Citron also describes how violent mobs threaten and target women with sexual violence, especially women with intersecting vulnerabilities. She explains the role of crowds in destroying privacy</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Online mob attacks against women include often-credible rape and death threats, with real-life targeting adding to their credibility. These attacks span multiple platforms and involve intense, sustained threats from many attackers in concerted and often coordinated campaigns. Violent mobs threaten and target women with sexual violence, especially women with intersecting vulnerabilities, and crowds play a role in destroying privacy.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_3542ae7e3ded23beb7969fb0__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
