    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID67",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_28817602f6e9200029cd72b3__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Avoiding stereotypes: Using different adjectives for women and men\n\nSometimes in English different adjectives are used to describe the same feature in women and men. There are also some words which – despite not having an explicit gender – have strong connotations that are strongly associated with only women or men.\n\nThis stems from the fact that some character traits, such as being ambitious, are considered by society attractive in men but negative in women.\n\nIt is not always easy to spot when adjectives are promoting gender stereotypes. The examples in the table below show some words to look out for and to avoid using to describe women.\n\n&gt; Tip!\n&gt;\n&gt; When describing a characteristic of a woman, ask yourself, ‘would I ever use this word to describe a man?’ (and vice versa) If not, then look for a term without gender connotations.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Different adjectives are sometimes used to describe the same feature in women and men, and some words without explicit gender carry strong connotations associated with only women or men. This reflects societal judgments that traits such as ambition are attractive in men but negative in women, so adjectives can promote gender stereotypes when used to describe women. A practical check is whether the same word would be used to describe a man; if not, a term without gender connotations should be sought.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_28817602f6e9200029cd72b3__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
        "DataVisibility": {
