    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID68",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "answer_relevance_card_28817602f6e9200029cd72b3__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>2.1 descriptive attacks – Characterising or describing women in a derogatory manner. This could include, but not limited to: negative generalisations about women’s abilities, appearance, sexual behaviour, intellect, character, or morals.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Different adjectives are sometimes used to describe the same feature in women and men, and some words without explicit gender carry strong connotations associated with only women or men. This reflects societal judgments that traits such as ambition are attractive in men but negative in women, so adjectives can promote gender stereotypes when used to describe women. A practical check is whether the same word would be used to describe a man; if not, a term without gender connotations should be sought.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "answer_relevance_card_28817602f6e9200029cd72b3__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
