    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID87",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_01f6e73c2168fef53d23f7e0__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">2. Conceptualising and defining cyber violence against women and girls &gt; Flaming\n\nFlaming is a form of ‘aggressive, hostile, profanity-laced’ (O’Sullivan and Flanagin, 2003) online communication, which is always characterised by ‘insults, negative affect and “typographic energy” such as capital letters and exclamation marks’ (Jane, 2015). It entails deliberately ‘swearing or using otherwise offensive language’ (Moor, Heuvelman and Verleur, 2010) to express emotionally charged or contrarian statements, usually to elicit a response from another online user (CSES, 2019).\n\nThis term appears mostly in scholarly work, where it is often considered an umbrella term for trolling, cyber bullying and cyber harassment. Very few mentions of flaming appear in national policies or laws.\n\nTends to occur in the context of online discussions about controversial issues (e.g. of a political, social, cultural or religious nature).\n\nCan be openly misogynous in nature and is often targeted at women with threats and/or fantasies of sexual violence or incitement to sexual violence (Andersen, 2021).</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Flaming is aggressive, hostile online communication characterised by insults, negative emotion, and emphatic typography such as capital letters and exclamation marks. It deliberately uses swearing or offensive language to express emotionally charged or contrarian statements, usually to provoke a response from another user. Flaming can be openly misogynous and is often directed at women through threats or fantasies of sexual violence, or incitement to sexual violence.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
