    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID119",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_084c2d057568ed4fbb9abb1e__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">YOUTH GUIDE TO END ONLINE GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE &gt; START WITH YOURSELF\n\nDon’t use hate speech, sexist or aggressive language online.\n\nDon’t keep sending messages when a woman has told you no. Recognise that no means no. Do not create multiple accounts to communicate with anyone, this is classified as online stalking.\n\nEducate yourself and others:\n\nBy taking the time to understand the nature, extent, and impact of online gender-based violence, men and boys can effectively educate themselves and others. They can share resources, participate in awareness campaigns, and engage in discussions about respectful online behaviour.\n\nInform yourself of the realities of men’s violence against women online.\n\nSUPPORT VICTIMS AND SURVIVORS:\n\nListen (or read), believe, respect and do not judge.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Men and boys are advised not to use hate speech, sexist or aggressive language online and to engage in discussions about respectful online behaviour. They can educate themselves and others about the nature, extent, and impact of online gender-based violence and inform themselves about the realities of men’s violence against women online. Supporting victims and survivors involves listening, believing, respecting, and not judging them.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_084c2d057568ed4fbb9abb1e__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
