    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID123",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_fa8d9af4aecfc601a3aa7eae__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Language inclusivity scale\n\nFor practical guidance, you can refer to a language inclusivity scale to understand what language should be outright avoided, considered and adopted to support greater gender equality.\n\nDefinition of key terms\n\nSexist language: when someone intentionally uses derogatory and discriminatory language against a particular gender.\n\nGender-biased language: such language either implicitly or explicitly favours one gender over another and is a form of gender discrimination.\n\nGender-neutral language: such language is not gender-specific and considers people in general with no reference to women or men.\n\nGender-inclusive language: an approach that places both women and men at the same level and that does not convey gender stereotypes.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">A language inclusivity scale distinguishes sexist language, defined as intentional derogatory and discriminatory language against a particular gender, from gender-biased language, which implicitly or explicitly favours one gender and is a form of gender discrimination. It also distinguishes gender-neutral language, which is not gender-specific and considers people in general, from gender-inclusive language, which places women and men at the same level and does not convey gender stereotypes.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_fa8d9af4aecfc601a3aa7eae__deepseek_deepseek-v4_1-flash-1017",
