    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID127",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_00b15fcad656474e2900c303__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">YOUTH GUIDE TO END ONLINE GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE &gt; START WITH YOURSELF\n\nDon’t use hate speech, sexist or aggressive language online.\n\nDon’t keep sending messages when a woman has told you no. Recognise that no means no. Do not create multiple accounts to communicate with anyone, this is classified as online stalking.\n\nEducate yourself and others:\n\nBy taking the time to understand the nature, extent, and impact of online gender-based violence, men and boys can effectively educate themselves and others. They can share resources, participate in awareness campaigns, and engage in discussions about respectful online behaviour.\n\nInform yourself of the realities of men’s violence against women online.\n\nSUPPORT VICTIMS AND SURVIVORS:\n\nListen (or read), believe, respect and do not judge.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Avoiding sexist language online is part of taking personal responsibility for ending online gender-based violence. Men and boys can educate themselves and others by learning about its nature, extent, and impact, sharing resources, participating in awareness campaigns, and discussing respectful online behaviour.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_00b15fcad656474e2900c303__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
