    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID147",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "faithfulness_card_a573092335c6542b5d6e7f08__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<strong>Paragraph</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Where does sexism come from?\n\n&gt; ### Definition: gender stereotypes\n&gt;\n&gt; Preconceived ideas whereby females and males are arbitrarily assigned characteristics and roles determined and limited by their gender.\n&gt;\n&gt; Source: EIGE.\n\nGender stereotypes underpin sexist behaviour and practices. Gender stereotypes do not need to be hostile to be harmful. Many workplace realities have been shaped by the belief that women and men have complementary characteristics, for example that men are individualistic and dominant, while women are caring and collaborative.\n\nHowever, stereotypically masculine characteristics are frequently valued more highly than stereotypically feminine characteristics (examples given below), although there is evidence this is changing. This is also true in the work context, where professional development books and programmes frequently urge women to stop displaying stereotypically feminine behaviour if they want to advance in their careers.</div><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes arbitrarily assign and limit people’s characteristics and roles based on gender, underpinning sexist behaviour and practices even when they are not hostile. Beliefs that men and women have complementary characteristics have shaped workplace realities, where stereotypically masculine traits are often valued more highly than stereotypically feminine traits. Professional development books and programmes frequently urge women to stop displaying stereotypically feminine behaviour to advance their careers, although there is evidence that the unequal valuation of these traits is changing.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the paragraph?</strong>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "faithfulness_card_a573092335c6542b5d6e7f08__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
