        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes – Asserting immutable, natural or otherwise essential differences between men and women. In some cases, this could be in the form of using women’s traits to attack men. Most sexist jokes will fall into this category.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Gender stereotypes arbitrarily assign and limit people’s characteristics and roles based on gender, underpinning sexist behaviour and practices even when they are not hostile. Beliefs that men and women have complementary characteristics have shaped workplace realities, where stereotypically masculine traits are often valued more highly than stereotypically feminine traits. Professional development books and programmes frequently urge women to stop displaying stereotypically feminine behaviour to advance their careers, although there is evidence that the unequal valuation of these traits is changing.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
