    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_9Kzg3KA9F3QnEFM",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID152",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "answer_relevance_card_62662f3df722a6c8f0d0b7a0__openai_gpt-6_1-sol",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<hr><br><strong>EDOS Label</strong><br><p>3.3 backhanded gendered compliments – Ostensibly complimenting women, but actually belittling or implying their inferiority. This could include, but is not limited to: reduction of women’s value to their attractiveness or sexual desirability, or implication that women are innately frail, helpless or weak.</p><br><strong>Argument</strong><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Language can trivialise women or things associated with women by making them sound ‘small’ or ‘cute’. Although such wording may appear benign, it can belittle women and reinforce their subordinate place in society and men’s traditional dominance over them.</div><br><strong>How relevant is the argument to the description of the EDOS label?</strong>",
